			0 0
		;;
	2)
		whiptail --title "Dictionary Size (-md) & RAM Usage" \
			--scrolltext --msgbox "\
Dictionary Size (-md) Explained
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

The -md flag sets the LZMA/LZMA2 dictionary size.
Think of it as how much previously-seen data the
compressor keeps \"in memory\" while looking for
repeated patterns.

⚠️  RAM WARNING
────────────────
The compressor needs roughly:
  Compression  :  ~12× the dictionary size
  Decompression:  ~1× the dictionary size

So -md=768M needs about 9 GB RAM to COMPRESS.
On an 8 GB machine this WILL cause swapping or
an out-of-memory kill!

DICTIONARY SIZE REFERENCE TABLE
───────────────────────────────
-md     Speed       RAM (compress)  Ratio
────────────────────────────────────────────
 64M    Faster      ~768 MB         Baseline
128M    Faster      ~1.5 GB         Better
256M    Fast        ~3 GB           Better
512M    Moderate    ~6 GB           Good
768M    Moderate    ~9 GB           Very good
  1G    Slower      ~12 GB          Slightly better
1.5G    Slower      ~18 GB          Slightly better
  2G+   Much slower ~24 GB+         Diminishing
────────────────────────────────────────────

KEY TAKEAWAYS
─────────────
• Larger dictionary ≠ automatically much smaller
  file. The gain depends on how far apart repeated
  patterns are in your data.

• For an 8 GB RAM machine:
  ✅  64M–256M   safe, comfortable
  ⚠️  512M       tight, close to limit
  ❌  768M+      will likely OOM or swap heavily

• For 16 GB RAM:  512M–768M is comfortable.
• For 32 GB+ RAM: 1G–1.5G is reasonable.

• If your machine starts swapping (disk thrashing),
  the compression will be MUCH slower than just
  using a smaller dictionary in the first place.

RECOMMENDATION
──────────────
Pick the largest dictionary that fits comfortably
in ~60-70% of your FREE RAM. Check with:
  free -h
before starting a big compression job." \
			0 0
		;;
	3)
		whiptail --title "Compression Settings Explained" \
			--scrolltext --msgbox "\
Compression Settings Reference
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

-mx (Compression Level)
───────────────────────
 1  = Fastest, largest output
 5  = Normal balance
 7  = Maximum
 9  = Ultra – slowest but smallest output

-m0 (Method)
────────────
 lzma2   Best ratio, multi-threaded (recommended)
 lzma    Classic, single-threaded
 ppmd    Good for plain text / source code
 bzip2   Moderate ratio, moderate speed
 deflate Zip-compatible, fastest

-mfb (Fast Bytes)
─────────────────
Range: 5 – 273
Higher = the compressor tries harder to find
optimal matches. 273 is the maximum and gives
the best ratio but is slower.
For most use cases, 64–128 is a good balance.
273 is for when you want absolute best ratio
and don't mind waiting.

-md (Dictionary Size)
─────────────────────
Sets the sliding dictionary size (e.g. 256M, 512M).
Remember: Compression RAM is ~12× the dict size!

-mmt (CPU Threads)
──────────────────
Sets multi-threading:
 • Empty / default : uses all available CPU threads.
 • -mmt=N (e.g. 2, 4): limits 7z to N threads so
   the rest of your system stays responsive.
 • -mmt=off        : single-threaded mode.

-p (Password Encryption)
────────────────────────
 Encrypts archive contents using AES-256.
 Example: -p\"MyPassword\"

-mhe (Header Encryption)
────────────────────────
 Encrypts archive headers / file names (7z format only):
 • -mhe=on  : Hides file names, sizes, and folder
              structure. The password is required even
              to list files inside the archive.
 • -mhe=off : Only file contents are encrypted; file
              names remain visible.

-ms (Solid Mode)
────────────────
 on  = Files are compressed as one continuous
       stream. Better ratio, but extracting a
       single file requires processing all
       preceding data.
 off = Each file is independent. Worse ratio,
       but random access is faster.

Use 'on' for archival / backup.
Use 'off' if you need to extract individual
files frequently." \
			0 0
		;;
	4)
		whiptail --title "Tips & Best Practices" \
			--scrolltext --msgbox "\
Tips & Best Practices
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

BEFORE COMPRESSING
──────────────────
1. Check available RAM:
     free -h
   Pick a dictionary size that fits in ~60-70%
   of your free memory.

2. Limit CPU threads (-mmt):
   If you need to use your computer while it
   compresses, set -mmt to 2 or 4 (or total cores - 2)
   to keep the desktop responsive and avoid high heat.

3. Encryption & Privacy:
   Always use header encryption (-mhe=on) so that
   file names and directory structures cannot be
   inspected without knowing the password.
   When typing in the dark, select 'Visible' mode to
   see keystrokes in real time and avoid mistyping.

4. Check available disk space:
     df -h /path/to/destination
   You need space for the archive AND the
   original (7z doesn't delete the source).

5. For very large files (10+ GB), consider
   running in a 'screen' or 'tmux' session so
   you can disconnect and reconnect:
     screen -S compress
     ./7z-profiler.sh
     # (Ctrl+A, D to detach)
     # screen -r compress  (to re-attach)

PROFILE TIPS
────────────
• Duplicate a profile to create variations
  (e.g. same source, different dict sizes to
  compare results).

• Profile names can use letters, numbers,
  hyphens, and underscores.

• Profiles are plain text files in:
    ~/.config/7z-profiler/profiles/
  You can back them up, copy to another
  machine, or edit with any text editor.

PERFORMANCE
───────────
• LZMA2 can use multiple CPU cores –
  it's almost always the best choice.

• If a job is too slow, try reducing:
  - Dictionary size (-md)  first
  - Fast bytes (-mfb)      second
  - Compression level (-mx) last

LOGS
────
Every compression run is logged to:
  ~/.config/7z-profiler/logs/
with timestamps. Use 'View logs' from the
main menu to review past runs. Passwords are
automatically masked in log files." \
			0 0
		;;
	esac
}

###############################################################################
# Main menu loop
###############################################################################

main_menu() {
	while true; do
		local choice
		choice=$(whiptail --title "7z Compression Profile Manager" \
			--menu "Choose an action:" \
			0 0 0 \
			"1" "🚀  Run a saved profile" \
			"2" "⚡  Quick run (one-off compression)" \
			"3" "🔒  Create encrypted archive (-mhe=on -p)" \
			"4" "➕  Create new profile" \
			"5" "✏️   Edit existing profile" \
			"6" "📋  View profile details" \
			"7" "📑  Duplicate a profile" \
			"8" "🗑️   Delete a profile" \
			"9" "📄  View compression logs" \
			"10" "❓  Help / About" \
			"0" "❌  Exit" \
			3>&1 1>&2 2>&3) || break

		case "$choice" in
		1) menu_run_profile ;;
		2) menu_quick_run ;;
		3) menu_encrypted_archive ;;
		4) menu_create_profile ;;
		5) menu_edit_profile ;;
		6) menu_view_profile ;;
		7) menu_duplicate_profile ;;
		8) menu_delete_profile ;;
		9) menu_view_logs ;;
		10) menu_help ;;
		0) break ;;
		esac
	done
}

###############################################################################
# Entry point
###############################################################################

# Pre-flight checks
for dep in whiptail 7z; do
	if ! command -v "$dep" &>/dev/null; then
		echo "ERROR: '$dep' is required but not found in PATH." >&2
		echo "Install it with:  sudo apt install ${dep/7z/p7zip-full}" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
done

main_menu
clear
unset NEWT_COLORS
echo "Goodbye!"
